Here's a complete example of a C# MCP server that wraps a REST API:

---

**Tools/WeatherTools.cs** — Tool class wrapping a REST API:

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class WeatherTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<WeatherTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get the current weather forecast for a city.")]
    public async Task<string> GetForecast(
        [Description("City name, e.g. 'Seattle' or 'London'")] string city,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching forecast for {City}", city);
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"/weather?city={Uri.EscapeDataString(city)}", cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Get the current temperature in Celsius for a city.")]
    public async Task<string> GetTemperature(
        [Description("City name")] string city,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var json = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"/temperature?city={Uri.EscapeDataString(city)}", cancellationToken);
        return json;
    }
}
```

**Program.cs** — stdio transport with DI:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: Log to stderr only — stdout is the JSON-RPC transport
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

// Register HttpClient with a base address for the API
builder.Services.AddHttpClient<WeatherTools>(client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.weatherapi.com/v1");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

## How DI Works for MCP Tools

The SDK supports two injection styles:

| Style | When to use |
|-------|-------------|
| **Constructor injection** (non-static class) | Tools that share services like `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>` |
| **Method parameter injection** (static class) | Simple tools needing one-off services |

**Key rule:** Any parameter whose type is registered in DI (or is `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<T>`) is **automatically injected** and **excluded from the tool's JSON schema**. Only parameters with `[Description]` are exposed to the LLM.

So in the example above, `HttpClient` and `ILogger<WeatherTools>` come from DI via the constructor, while `city` is the only parameter the LLM sees. The SDK resolves the tool class from the DI container on each invocation.

**To scaffold this project from scratch:**

```bash
dotnet new mcpserver -n WeatherMcpServer
cd WeatherMcpServer
```

Then replace the generated files with the code above and run with `dotnet run`.
